This `useEffect` responds to a user event (the composer losing focus) rather than handling it in the event handler. `isFocused` is only ever set to `false` inside the `onBlur` handler (`onBlur={() => setIsFocused(false)}`), so hiding the suggestion menu via an effect that watches `isFocused` adds an extra render cycle and obscures the direct relationship between the blur event and the response.

Call `hideSuggestionMenu()` directly from the blur handler and drop the effect:

```tsx
onBlur={() => {
    setIsFocused(false);
    hideSuggestionMenu();
}}
```

Then remove the `useEffect(() => { if (isFocused) { return; } hideSuggestionMenu(); }, [isFocused, hideSuggestionMenu]);` block.

This `useEffect` runs `debouncedCommentMaxLengthValidation(value)` in response to a user event: `value` only changes when the user types (`onChangeText={setValue}` on the `Composer`). Handling a user event inside a `useEffect` adds an extra render cycle and obscures the direct relationship between the typing action and the validation. The event handler (`onChangeText`) is already available, and the codebase's own composer already validates this way directly in the change handler (see `ComposerInput.tsx` `onValueChange`, which calls `debouncedCommentMaxLengthValidation?.(v)`).

Remove the effect and call the validation from a change handler wired to the `Composer` instead:

```tsx
const onChangeValue = (newValue: string) => {
    setValue(newValue);
    debouncedCommentMaxLengthValidation(newValue);
};

// ...
<Composer
    value={value}
    onChangeText={onChangeValue}
    // ...
/>
```

Then delete:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    debouncedCommentMaxLengthValidation(value);
}, [value, debouncedCommentMaxLengthValidation]);
```

`updateLastAccessedWorkspace(policyID)` is a side effect (`Onyx.set`) called directly in the component render body. This will execute on every render, including re-renders caused by unrelated state or prop changes, React Strict Mode double-invocations, and concurrent rendering retries. The previous `useEffect` approach was correct because it limited execution to when `policyID` actually changed.

Restore the `useEffect` wrapper:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!policyID) {
        return;
    }
    updateLastAccessedWorkspace(policyID);
}, [policyID]);
```

