The `useEffect` watching `focusPoint` state exists only to call `focusCamera` in response to a tap gesture event. Since the tap gesture handler already runs on JS thread (`.runOnJS(true)`), `focusCamera` can be called directly in `onStart` instead of going through `useState` + `useEffect`.

This eliminates an unnecessary render cycle on every tap and simplifies the code.

```tsx
// Remove the focusPoint state and useEffect, and call focusCamera directly:
.onStart((ev: {x: number; y: number}) => {
    const point = {x: ev.x, y: ev.y};

    focusIndicatorOpacity.set(withSequence(withTiming(0.8, {duration: 250}), withDelay(1000, withTiming(0, {duration: 250}))));
    focusIndicatorScale.set(2);
    focusIndicatorScale.set(withSpring(1, {damping: 10, stiffness: 200}));
    focusIndicatorPosition.set(point);

    focusCamera(camera, point);
});
```

